      "dom_id": "question-7595",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1?$",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=16$ and ${b}^{2}=9.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=16,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be  $a=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=9,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{9}=3.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=4,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=3.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 4=8,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 3=6.$",
